      "credit": "Hydrothermal quartz-gold vein (Mother Lode Gold Ore, California) 1",
      "source": "Mother Lode Gold OreHarvard mine quartz-gold vein",
      "description": "Hydrothermal quartz-gold vein sample (3.2 cm across) from the Harvard Open Pit Mine, northwestern side of Rt. 49/Rt. 108, western side of Woods Creek, just west of Jamestown, western Tuolumne County, Sierra Nevada foothills, central California, USA (37° 56’ 50” North , 120° 36’ 20” West).\nGeology - hydrothermal quartz-gold veins of the Mother Lode Gold Belt occur in the Melones Fault Zone (= boundary between the Foothills Terrane and the Don Pedro Terrane).  To the east of the fault zone is Jurassic slates.  To the west of the fault zone is serpentinite melange.\n\nAge - The Melones Fault Zone was active principally in the Late Jurassic and Early Cretaceous.  Gold-quartz mineralization occurred during the late Early Cretaceous, at about 108 to 127 Ma.",
